{"candidates":[{"claim":"There are exactly 648 three-digit numbers (100–999) with all three digits pairwise distinct.","mechanism":"Positional counting: 9 choices for the hundreds digit (1–9), 9 choices for the tens digit (0–9 excluding the hundreds digit), 8 choices for the units digit (excluding the two already chosen). Product: 9 × 9 × 8 = 648.","counterconditions":["If the problem instead required all digits nonzero, the count would be 9×8×7=504.","If zero were allowed as a leading digit (i.e., 000–999), the count would be 10×9×8=720.","If the problem required at least one repeated digit, the complement would be 900−648=252."],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SCR_001","SCR_002"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":1.0},{"claim":"There are exactly 648 three-digit numbers with all distinct digits, obtainable via inclusion-exclusion on the complement (repeated-digit numbers).","mechanism":"Total three-digit numbers: 900. Count the complement (at least one repeated digit) via PIE: numbers with hundreds=tens (9×10=90), hundreds=units (9×10=90), tens=units (9×10=90); subtract pairwise overlaps where all three equal (9). Complement = 90+90+90−9 = 252. Answer = 900−252 = 648.","counterconditions":["If the PIE overlap terms were miscounted (e.g., double-subtracting the triple-equality case), the complement would be wrong.","If only two of the three pairwise-repetition events were considered, the complement would be 180, yielding 720."],"optional_refs":["SRC_006","SCR_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"There are exactly 648 valid numbers, verified by splitting into zero-containing and zero-free cases.","mechanism":"Case A — zero appears in tens or units (but not hundreds): 9 hundreds choices × 1 zero position (tens or units) × 8 remaining nonzero choices for the other position = 9 × 2 × 8 = 144. Case B — no zero at all: 9 × 8 × 7 = 504. Total = 144 + 504 = 648.","counterconditions":["If Case A were omitted entirely (treating 'all distinct' as 'all nonzero'), the answer would be 504.","If Case A double-counted numbers with zero in both tens and units, it would overcount by 9 (but such numbers have a repeated zero, so they are excluded by distinctness and do not arise)."],"optional_refs":["SRC_007","SCR_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"There are 504 three-digit numbers with all distinct digits.","mechanism":"Treat the constraint as requiring all three digits to be nonzero and pairwise distinct: 9 choices for hundreds, 8 for tens, 7 for units, giving 9 × 8 × 7 = 504.","counterconditions":["If zero is allowed in tens or units (as the problem actually permits, since only the hundreds digit is constrained to be nonzero), this undercounts by 144 (the zero-containing distinct-digit numbers).","If the problem is reread as 'all three digits different from each other' without a nonzero requirement on tens/units, the correct answer is 648."],"optional_refs":["SRC_008","SCR_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"claim":"There are 648 three-digit numbers with all distinct digits, confirmed by summing per-hundred-block counts.","mechanism":"For each hundreds digit h (1–9), count valid (tens, units) pairs with both different from h and from each other. There are 9 valid tens digits (0–9 excluding h) and then 8 valid units digits (0–9 excluding h and the chosen tens digit): 9 × 8 = 72 per block. Nine blocks: 9 × 72 = 648.","counterconditions":["If the per-block count assumed tens cannot be zero, each block would yield 8×7=56, totaling 504.","If the per-block count failed to exclude h from the units choices, each block would yield 9×9=81, totaling 729."],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SCR_002"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"There are 648 valid numbers, validated by a filtering automaton over all 900 three-digit numbers.","mechanism":"Enumerate all integers 100–999 (900 values). Apply a filter: accept n iff the hundreds digit h, tens digit t, and units digit u satisfy h≠t, h≠u, t≠u. By symmetry each hundreds digit contributes exactly 72 valid (t, u) pairs, so the automaton accepts 9×72=648.","counterconditions":["If the automaton's filter required h, t, u all nonzero, it would accept 504.","If the automaton's filter only checked h≠t and h≠u (omitting t≠u), it would accept 9×9×9=729."],"optional_refs":["SRC_007","SCR_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7}]}